{"name":"Crystal Palace","hex":"#d3cfab","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Crystal Palace","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An area mainly in the borough of Bromley, south-east Greater London, England, named after the \"palace\" that was moved there after being erected for The Great Exhibition of 1851 ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Crystal_Palace"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crystal Palace Amusement Park","blocks":[{"text":"Crystal Palace was an indoor amusement park located near Moncton in Dieppe, New Brunswick, Canada. Owned and operated by Cadillac Fairview, the indoor park was situated adjacent to the Champlain Place shopping mall and originally opened to the public on February 15, 1990. It was part of a larger complex that included the Ramada Plaza Crystal Palace Hotel and Convention Centre, as well as other shops and restaurants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Crystal_Palace_in_2014_2014-08-10_%28levels_and_filter%29.jpg/330px-Crystal_Palace_in_2014_2014-08-10_%28levels_and_filter%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal_Palace_Amusement_Park","wikidata":"Q3360850"},{"title":"Crystal","blocks":[{"text":"A crystal or crystalline solid is a solid material whose constituents are arranged in a highly ordered microscopic structure, forming a crystal lattice that extends in all directions. In addition, macroscopic single crystals are usually identifiable by their geometrical shape, consisting of flat faces with specific, characteristic orientations. The scientific study of crystals and crystal formation is known as crystallography. The process of crystal formation via mechanisms of crystal growth is called crystallization or solidification."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Quartz_28.jpg/330px-Quartz_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal","wikidata":"Q43533"},{"title":"Palace","blocks":[{"text":"A palace is a large residence, often serving as a royal residence or the home for a head of state or another high-ranking dignitary, such as a bishop or archbishop. The word is derived from the Latin word palātium, for Palatine Hill in Rome, which housed the Imperial residences."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Palacio_Real_de_Madrid_Julio_2016_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Palacio_Real_de_Madrid_Julio_2016_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace","wikidata":"Q16560"},{"title":"The Crystal Palace","blocks":[{"text":"The Crystal Palace was a cast iron and plate glass structure, originally built in Hyde Park, London, to house the Great Exhibition of 1851. The exhibition took place from 1 May to 15 October 1851, and more than 14,000 exhibitors from around the world gathered in its 990,000-square-foot (92,000 m2) exhibition space to display examples of technology developed in the Industrial Revolution. Designed by Joseph Paxton, the Great Exhibition building was 1,851 feet (564 m) long, with an interior height of 128 feet (39 m), and was three times the size of St Paul's Cathedral."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Crystal_Palace_General_view_from_Water_Temple.jpg/330px-Crystal_Palace_General_view_from_Water_Temple.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Crystal_Palace","wikidata":"Q202902"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Crystal_Palace_in_2014_2014-08-10_%28levels_and_filter%29.jpg/500px-Crystal_Palace_in_2014_2014-08-10_%28levels_and_filter%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu